WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.067
Lesson 5 is richer because it asks students to hold two ideas at once.

2
00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:12.135
Pressure is a local condition inside the fluid.

3
00:00:12.135 --> 00:00:18.202
It tells us how intensely the fluid is pushing at a particular place.

4
00:00:18.202 --> 00:00:24.269
Force, however, is the total push on an actual area that we choose on a wall, base, gate, or curved surface.

5
00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:30.336
That is why a question can keep the same pressure at one location yet produce different total forces on different sized patches.

6
00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:36.404
The worked example becomes much cleaner once students stop treating pressure and force as interchangeable words.

7
00:00:36.404 --> 00:00:42.471
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson starts to click when pressure is read as intensity and force is read as the resulting push on a selected area.

8
00:00:42.471 --> 00:00:48.538
Local quantity means pressure at a point.

9
00:00:48.538 --> 00:00:54.605
Pressure describes the squeeze intensity where the fluid is.

10
00:00:54.605 --> 00:01:00.673
Surface quantity means force on an area.

11
00:01:00.673 --> 00:01:06.740
Force depends on how much surface is being pushed at that pressure.

12
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:13.517
The diagram is especially important here because it corrects two common mistakes at once.

13
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:20.293
First, it shows that a fluid does not somehow push only downward or only sideways.

14
00:01:20.293 --> 00:01:27.070
It pushes against every surface it touches, and the local force is normal to that surface.

15
00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:33.847
Second, it shows why the chosen patch matters.

16
00:01:33.847 --> 00:01:40.623
If the local pressure is known, doubling the area doubles the total force collected by that patch.

17
00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:47.400
Students who can read both ideas from the picture are ready for the worked example logic.

18
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.177
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong pressure-force diagram shows both the area dependence of force and the surface-normal direction rule.

19
00:01:54.177 --> 00:02:00.953
At one location: The fluid has one local pressure value there, regardless of which patch you later select.

20
00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:07.730
Choose a patch: The total pressure force depends on how much area is being acted on.

21
00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:14.191
Imagine a curved wall made of many tiny tiles, and imagine each tile being met by a tiny pusher that always stands square to that tile.

22
00:02:14.191 --> 00:02:20.652
No matter how the wall curves, each little pusher acts perpendicular to its own patch.

23
00:02:20.652 --> 00:02:27.113
If the pressure is greater, every tiny pusher presses harder.

24
00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:33.574
If the patch area is larger, more of those tiny pushes are gathered together and the total force grows.

25
00:02:33.574 --> 00:02:40.035
That picture is useful because it keeps students from drawing one wrong global arrow for an entire curved surface.

26
00:02:40.035 --> 00:02:46.496
Keep this takeaway in view: The tile analogy makes it easier to trust that local surface direction matters just as much as the pressure value.

27
00:02:46.496 --> 00:02:52.957
Tile view means local pushes.

28
00:02:52.957 --> 00:02:59.418
Pressure can be imagined as many tiny pushes spread across surface patches.

29
00:02:59.418 --> 00:03:05.879
Direction view means square to the tile.

30
00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:12.340
Each tiny push acts perpendicular to the local patch.

31
00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:17.584
The key formula here is F equals p times A.

32
00:03:17.584 --> 00:03:22.828
The pressure part tells us the push intensity at the location.

33
00:03:22.828 --> 00:03:28.072
The area part tells us how much surface is collecting that push.

34
00:03:28.072 --> 00:03:33.315
The result is the total force due to pressure on the chosen patch.

35
00:03:33.315 --> 00:03:38.559
This is why students must resist saying pressure and force are the same thing.

36
00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:43.803
They are linked, but the area sits between them.

37
00:03:43.803 --> 00:03:49.047
And when drawing or describing the final force, the direction must be given as normal to the surface.

38
00:03:49.047 --> 00:03:54.291
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula works best when students say aloud what each symbol means physically before multiplying.

39
00:03:54.291 --> 00:03:59.535
If p stays fixed: A larger patch produces a larger total force.

40
00:03:59.535 --> 00:04:04.778
If A stays fixed: A larger local pressure produces a larger total force.

41
00:04:04.778 --> 00:04:10.022
F = pA.

42
00:04:10.022 --> 00:04:15.266
pressure is the local intensity.

43
00:04:15.266 --> 00:04:20.510
After calculating F, state that the force acts perpendicular to the chosen surface.

44
00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.838
Use a three-step routine.

45
00:04:25.838 --> 00:04:31.166
First, identify the local pressure where the patch sits.

46
00:04:31.166 --> 00:04:36.495
In a liquid this may come from depth, or from atmospheric plus liquid pressure if the system is open.

47
00:04:36.495 --> 00:04:41.823
Second, choose the actual patch area named in the question.

48
00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:47.151
Third, multiply pressure by area to get the total force, and then state that the force acts normal to the surface.

49
00:04:47.151 --> 00:04:52.479
This sequence is powerful because it keeps the local pressure idea separate from the surface-force calculation until the very end.

50
00:04:52.479 --> 00:04:57.807
Keep this takeaway in view: Separating pressure first and force second removes most of the confusion in pressure-on-surface problems.

51
00:04:57.807 --> 00:05:03.135
Step 1 means find p.

52
00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:08.464
Read the pressure at the location before touching the area.

53
00:05:08.464 --> 00:05:13.792
Step 2 means choose A.

54
00:05:13.792 --> 00:05:19.120
Use the patch area given or implied by the surface.

55
00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.325
Run four quick checks.

56
00:05:21.325 --> 00:05:23.530
If the pressure stays the same and the area doubles, does the force double?

57
00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.735
Yes.

58
00:05:25.735 --> 00:05:27.940
If the area stays the same and the pressure rises, does the force rise?

59
00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.145
Yes.

60
00:05:30.145 --> 00:05:32.350
Does pressure force act tangent to the surface?

61
00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.555
No, it acts normal to the surface.

62
00:05:34.555 --> 00:05:36.760
And if two different sized patches sit at the same location in the fluid, do they have the same total force automatically?

63
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.965
No, because the local pressure may match while the areas differ.

64
00:05:38.965 --> 00:05:41.170
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once students can keep the local pressure value separate from the total force on a surface.

65
00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.375
Same p, bigger A means bigger F.

66
00:05:43.375 --> 00:05:45.580
The surface collects more total push.

67
00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.785
Same A, bigger p means bigger F.

68
00:05:47.785 --> 00:05:49.990
Greater pressure intensity means greater force.

69
00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:56.129
You are ready for the worked example now.

70
00:05:56.129 --> 00:06:02.268
Begin by locating the patch and deciding what pressure exists there.

71
00:06:02.268 --> 00:06:08.408
Then read the patch area carefully and use F equals pA.

72
00:06:08.408 --> 00:06:14.547
Do not forget the direction statement: the pressure force is normal to the surface.

73
00:06:14.547 --> 00:06:20.686
If the diagram is curved, trust the local patch orientation rather than the overall shape of the container.

74
00:06:20.686 --> 00:06:26.825
The most secure answers explain why the pressure belongs to the location and why the force belongs to the chosen patch.

75
00:06:26.825 --> 00:06:32.964
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: students can now turn a local pressure into a correctly directed force on a real surface.

76
00:06:32.964 --> 00:06:39.103
Reading move: Separate location information from area information before calculating.

77
00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:45.242
Direction move: Draw or describe the arrow at right angles to the surface patch.

78
00:06:45.242 --> 00:06:51.382
find the pressure at the patch location.

79
00:06:51.382 --> 00:06:57.521
use the patch area in F = pA.

80
00:06:57.521 --> 00:07:03.660
A complete solution gives the local pressure story before the final force statement.
